<Layouts.app flash={@flash}>
  <h1>Terms</h1>
  <p class="mono quiet" id="updated">last updated {@updated}</p>
  <p class="lede">
    AI in Practice is a framework from DSC Ltd. These terms say what it is, what it is not, and how to use it fairly.
  </p>

  <h2>A conversation starter</h2>
  <p id="conversation-starter">
    An AI model holds a conversation with you about how you work with AI and writes two reports from it. The reports describe what you said against a published rubric. They give no score, no rank and no pass mark, and they are not a judgement of you as a person or of your suitability for any role.
  </p>

  <h2>Not an automated decision</h2>
  <p id="not-a-decision">
    Nothing here decides anything about anyone. The framework makes no decision about you, and it must not be used as the basis of one. Under UK GDPR (Article 22) people have the right not to be subject to a decision based solely on automated processing that significantly affects them. Anyone who reads an assessor report must treat it as one input to their own human judgement, alongside everything else they know, and talk with the person before reaching any view.
  </p>

  <h2>Identity is not checked</h2>
  <p>
    The framework does not check who took part. An assessor report says what was said in one conversation, by whoever used the link.
  </p>

  <h2>Fairness and bias</h2>
  <p id="fairness">
    AI models can carry bias, and the way someone writes can be shaped by their first language, background, disability, sector or the size of the organisation they work in. The rubric asks the model to credit context and to judge substance rather than polish, and it is checked for these effects before launch, but no one can rule them out. Anyone using a report in a hiring or appointment decision remains responsible for that decision under the Equality Act 2010, and should weigh the report with that in mind.
  </p>

  <h2>Your reports</h2>
  <p>
    Your own report is yours. The assessor report is signed, so anyone can confirm it has not been changed since it was written, and it goes to a recruiter or anyone else only if you send it yourself. DSC never sends it. Nothing is kept once the session ends, so download what you want before closing the page.
  </p>

  <h2>Using the framework fairly</h2>
  <ul id="fair-use">
    <li>Links are personal and work once. Please do not share or resell them.</li>
    <li>
      Answer in your own words. Trying to instruct the model rather than answer it is ignored, and the assessor report says it happened.
    </li>
    <li>
      Do not include other people's personal details, or anything confidential you are not free to share.
    </li>
    <li>Do not try to disrupt the service or get at anyone else's session.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    DSC may withdraw links or stop offering the framework at any time. It is offered free of charge and as it is: DSC does its best to keep it accurate and available, but makes no promise that it will be, and is not liable for decisions others make using a report, to the extent the law allows.
  </p>

  <h2>Privacy</h2>
  <p>
    How your answers are handled, and who else sees them on the way, is set out in the <.link href={
      ~p"/privacy"
    }>privacy notice</.link>.
  </p>

  <h2>The law</h2>
  <p>These terms are governed by the law of England and Wales.</p>
</Layouts.app>
